cadence_sdhci: allow byte/halfword HRS register access

cadence_sdhci_ops restricted ALL accesses to the Cadence-specific HRS
register window to exactly 4 bytes (.valid.min_access_size = 4). Real
Cadence SD4HC HRS registers are plain APB registers and accept byte
and halfword accesses too, and real low-level drivers rely on that --
e.g. Axera's vendor BL1 (boot/bl1/driver/mmc/sdhci_cdns.c) does
readb/readw as well as readl against these same offsets, HRS06
(eMMC control / tuning) in particular, which it accesses as a 16-bit
register via sdhci_cdns_readw()/writew().

With the old {4,4} constraint, any narrower access was rejected as
invalid by QEMU's memory core and surfaced to the guest as an
external abort -- for a real BL1 built from vendor source doing a
completely ordinary 16-bit register read, this is indistinguishable
from a genuine hardware fault: no -d unimp trace (the region exists
and is otherwise functional), just a synchronous abort into whatever
exception vector the guest has installed (or hasn't -- BL1 never
installs one this early, so it's a silent, unrecoverable trap).

Fix: widen .valid to {1,4}. .impl stays at {4,4} -- QEMU's generic
sub-word access synthesis (read-modify-write through the existing
4-byte-granular callback) handles narrower guest accesses correctly
without any change to cadence_sdhci_read()/write() themselves, since
both already operate on a uint32_t regs[] array indexed at 4-byte
granularity.

Found debugging why Axera's real vendor BL1 crashed inside
mmc_select_mode() (a routine 16-bit read/modify/write of HRS06) right
after successfully completing full eMMC card bring-up (CMD0 through
CMD7) against this project's own axera-cadence.sdhci wiring --
single-stepped the guest with gdb down to the exact faulting
instruction (a readw() inside mmc_select_mode whose very next
executed instruction after the load is the guest's own [unfixed]
exception vector, confirmed by resolving the LR back through nm
against a debug-symbol build of the real vendor BL1), then confirmed
via QEMU's monitor `xp /4wx` that the *address* itself reads fine with
a 4-byte access -- isolating the bug to access *size*, not address
mapping.

Verified: with this patch, the same BL1 binary now gets all the way
through mmc_select_mode and into reading real data blocks off the
(emulated) eMMC card, reaching verify_img_header() and correctly
reporting a header-magic mismatch against a blank test image ("spl
verify header failed") -- the expected, correct outcome for a card
with no real boot image flashed to it, not a crash.
diff --git a/hw/sd/cadence_sdhci.c b/hw/sd/cadence_sdhci.c
index d576855..297e0dd 100644
--- a/hw/sd/cadence_sdhci.c
+++ b/hw/sd/cadence_sdhci.c
@@ -127,8 +127,20 @@ static const MemoryRegionOps cadence_sdhci_ops = {
         .min_access_size = 4,
         .max_access_size = 4,
     },
+    /*
+     * Real Cadence SD4HC HRS registers (and real low-level drivers --
+     * e.g. Axera's vendor BL1, whose sdhci_cdns.c does readb/readw as
+     * well as readl against these same offsets, HRS06 in particular)
+     * accept byte and halfword APB accesses, not just 32-bit ones.
+     * With this restricted to {4,4}, any narrower access (a real
+     * driver's plain 16-bit read of a 16-bit-wide logical field like
+     * HRS06's tuning controls) was rejected as invalid and surfaced to
+     * the guest as an external abort -- QEMU's own sub-word access
+     * synthesis (read-modify-write through the .impl-sized callback)
+     * handles the splitting correctly once this allows it.
+     */
     .valid = {
-        .min_access_size = 4,
+        .min_access_size = 1,
         .max_access_size = 4,
     }
 };
